        "id": 238143,
        "name": "",
        "username": "The_Justifier",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/the_justifier/{size}/4954_2.png",
        "created_at": "2021-06-04T22:29:29.829Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Wait, I’m a little confused.  Adam and Christopher said in podcast 75 that they had played a bunch of Prime War.  That was in 2018!  If it was already a playable game that long ago, how was it unable to be put on the market within the next two years.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"never mind since that's basically already happening\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n never mind since that's basically already happening:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>And if you decided that it couldn’t be manufactured and sold on a commercial basis anyway, but it was a fully playable game, why not just publish it for free and let the fans make it for themselves?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Designing the game completely and then shelving it seems like the worst of two worlds, I don’t quite understand.  (EDIT:  Okay I understand a little, having read the whole thread and the Loreforge blog post.  Still seems odd they wouldn’t just publish a finished game, even if it wasn’t ideal, so they could at least recoup <em>some</em> funding from all their efforts.<br>\nIf they did a print-on-demand thing they could avoid any risk of overstock.)</p>",
        "post_number": 74,
        "post_type": 1,
